              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "bù hǎoyìsi — \"excuse me; sorry to trouble you\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "bù hǎoyìsi wǒ xiǎng wèn nǐ — Excuse me, could I ask you something?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "bù keeps its own falling tone here, because hǎo after it is a third tone, not a fourth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "bù hǎoyìsi is built from bù 'not', hǎo 'good' and yìsi 'meaning', all inherited from Middle Chinese, pjut, xawX, 'iH and si (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription); the dictionary glosses the last two as 'intention' and 'to think of'. 'Not a good feeling' is the literal sense; how it became an apology is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
